Python 3 Migration Notes
========================

This document describes the in-progress Python 3 migration of TMDA
1.1.12. The legacy code, historical copyright notices, license and
existing release identifier are preserved.

Reference implementations
-------------------------

Two independent repositories were reviewed for migration ideas:

* Jonathan Kamens: https://github.com/jikamens/tmda
* Cedric Dufour: https://github.com/cedric-dufour/tmda

The Kamens branch has useful subprocess substitutions but still
imports asyncore, asynchat, md5 and crypt in major components. It
must not be treated as proof of compatibility with Python 3.13+.

The Dufour tmda-1.3.x branch places TMDA in a tmda/ subtree and
includes an extended test suite, an explicit msg_as_bytes helper
and SMTP session byte decoding. It also retains asyncore, asynchat
and crypt, so it is not a direct drop-in replacement on a stock
Python 3.13+ installation.

Compatibility work completed
----------------------------

* HMAC-SHA1 API and original cryptographic cookie representations.
* Python 3-safe text and binary serialization of selected data.
* Removed popen2 functionality in selected utility, parser and
  authentication paths.
* Legacy filter parser string methods and indentation issues.
* Key generation, authentication encoding and CLI smoke tests.
* Python 3 standard-library email instead of the bundled Python 2
  email package on the entry-point path.
* Temporary pyasyncore and pyasynchat dependency for ofmipd imports.
* Optional legacycrypt fallback for encrypted Unix password files.

Compatibility work still required
---------------------------------

* End-to-end SMTP transactions, SASL, TLS and proxy transport.
* Real Maildir and original pending-queue delivery and retrieval.
* Message serialization, MIME and non-ASCII header/body handling.
* FD3/checkpassword process execution and failure handling.
* Compatibility of old file-based password hashes and security policy.
* Integration with supported MTAs and virtual domains.
* Full coverage of bundled helpers, contrib scripts and legacy
  configuration paths.

Existing smoke tests verify command-line startup and compilation,
but do not establish that the SMTP proxy is safe for production.

Migration policy
----------------

Preserve original behavior and external formats unless a separate,
reviewed compatibility or security change is required. Prefer
isolated regression tests and staged integration testing over a
bulk source rewrite. Keep source comments and documentation in
English, consistent with the original project.


SMTP loopback integration checks
-------------------------------

The integration/test_ofmipd_loopback.py suite starts the original
SMTPServer bound to 127.0.0.1 on an ephemeral port, using a callback
that captures messages in memory instead of invoking a local or remote
MTA. The GitHub Actions runner tests Python 3.13 and Python 3.14 with
temporary pyasyncore and pyasynchat compatibility packages.

The test suite covers EHLO, NOOP, QUIT, rejection before AUTH,
AUTH LOGIN failure and success, AUTH PLAIN success, MAIL FROM, RCPT TO,
DATA, 8-bit message content and SMTP dot transparency.

The checks do not cover STARTTLS, implicit TLS, real password stores,
FD3/checkpassword, message injection, external MTA delivery,
concurrent connections, or sustained SMTP traffic.

